<section
  id="case-portfolio-design"
  class="fullscreen-section case-study-section"
  data-title="Case Study: Katja Dev Portfolio — Web &amp; Interaction Design | Katja Turnsek"
  data-description="UX/UI and interaction design case study for a creative portfolio, focused on visual identity, motion, responsive structure, and clear project discovery."
  data-case="portfolio"
  aria-labelledby="case-portfolio-design-title"
>
  <div class="rows">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-header">
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Web &amp; Interaction Design</p>
        <h1 id="case-portfolio-design-title">Katja Dev Portfolio</h1>
        <p class="case-subtitle">A personal design system where fluid motion and
          expressive details support a clear presentation of multidisciplinary
          work.</p>

        <dl class="case-summary" aria-label="Project summary">
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI, interaction design, and art direction</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent portfolio project</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025–2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Figma, responsive prototyping, motion direction, design system</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Evolving portfolio identity</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class="tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper">
          <div
            class="demo-links"
            data-auto="demo"
            data-case="portfolio-design"
          ></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class="project-switcher"
          aria-label="Katja Dev Portfolio sections"
          data-auto="switcher"
          data-case="portfolio"
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The portfolio needed to represent both sides of my work: a long
        visual-design background and practical front-end development. I wanted
        it to feel recognisably personal and creative while still making
        projects, skills, and contact information easy for employers and clients
        to understand.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            A highly expressive portfolio can easily make its own interface
            louder than the work it presents. The central challenge was deciding
            where motion and personality add meaning, and where the design
            should step back to support reading and comparison.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Unify development, interaction design, and graphic design
              without flattening them</li>
            <li>Use motion as a signature without making long pages tiring</li>
            <li>Create a visual system that works in both light and dark themes</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-portfoliomoodboard-600.webp"
            data-src="design-portfoliomoodboard-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="515"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Katja Dev Portfolio mood board showing colours, typography, water, and movement references"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Mood board connecting water,
            adaptability, and creative technology</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Built the visual language around water and adaptability, expressed
              through organic shapes, wave lines, and fluid transitions rather
              than literal illustrations
            </li>
            <li>
              Paired expressive display typography with a restrained body
              typeface so section titles carry personality while case studies
              remain readable
            </li>
            <li>
              Used colour and motion to connect navigation states, theme
              changes, and project categories across the site
            </li>
            <li>
              Reserved the strongest animation for entry points and major
              headings, allowing longer project content to feel calmer
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-ktportfolio-600.webp"
            data-src="design-ktportfolio-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="503"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Katja Dev Portfolio wireframes showing the planned page structure and content flow"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Wireframes defining content rhythm
            before animation and visual styling</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The portfolio now works as both a project archive and an example of my
        interaction-design approach. Its identity is distinctive, but the system
        can continue growing as stronger work replaces older cases. The most
        useful lesson has been learning when to simplify: motion is more
        memorable when it has room to stand out rather than appearing beneath
        every piece of content.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="back-to-work-container">
      <a
        href="{{base}}#work"
        class="label-link back-to-work-label"
        data-back
        data-back-fallback="{{base}}#work"
        aria-label="Back to Work"
      >
        <svg class="icon icon-18 arrow-icon" aria-hidden="true"><use
            href="#i-arrow-left"
          /></svg>
        <span class="label-text">Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>